  [FAIL] V3a  the 10^320 margin is ROBUST to the freedom the calibrating data leave in the kernel   (IT IS NOT, and by a very large factor.  The tightest planet (Mars) is saturated by a 1/y relic with eps = 1.22e-05, which at y = 1 -- the middle of the RAR's own transition, where SPARC has thousands of points -- is a fractional change in nu of 7.71e-06.  The RAR's measured orthogonal scatter is 0.11 dex = 29% (McGaugh+2016), so this deformation is 4e+04x SMALLER than the noise on the data that fixed the kernel.  The 10^320 to 10^8900 is not a statement about the framework's robustness; it is the value of exp(-sqrt y) continued 94x-2319x beyond the largest sqrt(y) any fitting datum reaches.  The physically supportable statement is the DIRECTION (this functional form carries no isotropic planetary tail), never the adverb ('absolutely', 'at any conceivable precision, now or ever'))
  [PASS] V3b  the solar system is nevertheless a real 1e-5-level constraint ON THE KERNEL, which is the content the margin actually has   (the correct reading of the same arithmetic, stated as a measurement rather than as a margin: the planets bound any 1/y relic in nu to eps < 1.2e-05, i.e. they pin the kernel at the 8e-06 fractional level in a regime the galaxy data cannot see at all.  That is a genuine and quotable result and it is the SAME arithmetic; it is simply not the claim A2a makes)

